WHAT WAS TAKEN

l1b3rt4s_attacks.py constructs the framing variants this project refers to as
the plinius_* family, together with the distilled clean_* variants. It is a
reimplementation of techniques documented in that corpus. Two of the framings
retained in the final design space contain substantial reproduced text:

    plinius_full_openai     derived from OPENAI.mkd; measured against the
                            corpus, longest shared run 70 words, token
                            Jaccard 0.828, sequence ratio 0.881
    plinius_format_hijack   longest shared run 50 words, token Jaccard 0.699,
                            sequence ratio 0.794

A third, clean_openai, shares a 17-word run at Jaccard 0.337 and is described
in the registry as a distilled variant.

The remaining framings retained in the design space were measured against the
same corpus and share runs of eight words or fewer:

    plinius_l33tspeak 8, plinius_persona 5, libra_semantic_inv 3,
    libra_style 1, and the struct_* family 1 to 2

WHAT WAS CHANGED

The prompts are parameterised to accept an arbitrary instruction, are assembled
through a single class with fixed rather than randomised choices, and are
rendered through each target's own chat template. Per-framing provenance notes
are recorded in attacks/framing_registry.py.

HOW THIS WAS MEASURED

compare_framing_provenance.py in this repository reproduces the figures above
against a clone of the corpus. It reports similarity scores only and prints no
prompt text.

